INFO:agent.loop:\u26a1 Executing tool: 'knowledge_agent' with arguments: {"source_title": "What are the wider supervisory implications of the Wirecard case?", "source_url": "https://safe-frankfurt.de/fileadmin/user_upload/editor_common/Policy_Center/SAFE_Policy_White_Paper_74.pdf", "text": "SRC-002, an EU Economic Governance Support Unit policy briefing (completed May 2020; published November 2020), states that all five lines of defence against corporate fraud/deception failed in one way or another: Wirecard's internal control system, supervisory board, external audit, oversight bodies for financial reporting and auditing, and market supervisor BaFin. It says signals suggesting fraudulent behaviour existed years before revelation. The briefing reports allegations beginning in 2015, a 2016 Zatarra report alleging money laundering and fraud, a January 2019 BaFin-requested FREP investigation, and continuing Financial Times reporting. It states KPMG was hired in October 2019; its report, delayed until April 2020, said KPMG was unable to verify the existence of €1 billion in foreign bank accounts. It states BaFin filed a criminal complaint in June 2020 and, a few days later, Wirecard filed for bankruptcy acknowledging €1.9 billion in missing financial assets. The briefing identifies weaknesses in whistleblower/information flows, external audits/internal controls/market forces, supervisory investigation and enforcement powers, and fragmented European oversight. It expressly cautions against hindsight bias and frames some conclusions as the authors' assessment."}
